      "title": "Calculating a Directional Derivative"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 4 y$ at the point $(-3,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=3\\,\\mathbf{i}-1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(3\\,\\mathbf{i}-1\\,\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 4 y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 4 y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x,4⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-3,1) & =⟨2 (-3),4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(-3,1) & =∇f(-3,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨-6,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10},- \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (3)+(4)\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
